    "evidence_text": "Low scores indicate low novelty for the entity profile INLINEFORM10 .\n\nWe only report the evaluation metrics for the `relevant' news-entity pairs.\n\nBaselines.\n\nB1.\n\nThe first baseline uses only the salience-based\n\nfeatures by Dunietz and Gillick  .\n\nLearning Models.\n\nWe use Random Forests (RF)  .\n\nThe higher the margin score the more robust the model.\n\nMetrics.\n\nPerformance.\n\nThe results show the precision\u2013recall curve.\n\nWe measure the significance through the t-test statistic and get a p-value of\n\nINLINEFORM1 .\n\nRobustness.\n\npre-computed templates.\n\nWith the\n\nprecision of P=0.17, with a uniform distribution across the years.\n\nThe results are already stable for the year range INLINEFORM2 .\n\nThese additionally represent classes with a sufficient number of training instances for the years\n\nThe lowest F1 score is for the Criminal and Television classes.\n\nHowever, this is directly correlated with the insufficient number of instances.\n\nThe baseline approaches for the ASP task perform poorly.\n\nFor different entity classes different features and combination of features is necessary.\n\nThe last analysis is the impact we have on expanding entity profiles\n\nResults suggest that the news content cited in entity pages comes from the first paragraphs.",
